L538: To obtain an integrated measure that jointly accounts for true positive rate (TPR) and false positive rate (FPR), we further report logAUC. Concretely, we scan the FPR on a logarithmic scale from $10^{-12}$ to $10^{-1}$ and compute the area under the resulting ROC curve. The visualization in cite261†Fig. 6 shows that our OSI method consistently outperforms GS in logAUC across all $f_{hw}$ configurations and adversarial scenarios, further demonstrating the superiority of OSI.
L539: ### C.2 Advanced Attack
L540: 
L541: Beyond the standard distortion-based evaluation, we also investigate the robustness of OSI under more advanced attacks. In particular, we consider two families of attacks: VAE-based compression attacks and embedding-based adversarial attacks. cite262†Tab. 19 details the quantitative performance of OSI under these advanced attack scenarios.
L542: The first family consists of attacks based on variational autoencoder (VAE) image compression models. Given a watermarked image $I$, the VAE encodes $I$ into a latent representation, applies quantization to induce loss, and then reconstructs the latent back to pixel space. The overall compression–reconstruction process can be expressed as
L543: 
L544:  | $$\hat{I}_{q}=\mathcal{D}\!\left(\mathcal{Q}_{q}\!\left(\mathcal{E}(I)\right)\right),$$  |  | (12)
L545: where $\mathcal{E}$ and $\mathcal{D}$ denote VAE encoder and VAE decoder, and $\mathcal{Q}_{q}(\cdot)$ is a quantization operator which is controlled by the quality parameter $q$. We evaluate six VAE-based compression models [cite118†1 , cite119†36 , cite120†7 ] at their strongest attack setting (quality level $q=1$). As shown in cite262†Tab. 19 , OSI consistently outperforms GS under all compression attacks.
L546: The second family, embedding attack, aims to perturb the input image such that its representation under a fixed encoder is significantly altered, while the perturbed image remains visually similar to the original in pixel space. Let $f(\cdot)$ denote a pretrained embedding model. Given a watermarked image $I$, we first compute its embedding $f(I)$ and then seek an adversarial image $I_{adv}$ that is close to $I$ in pixel space but yields a substantially different embedding $f(I_{adv})$.
L547: Formally, the objective is defined as:
L548:  | $$\underset{I_{adv}}{\max}\;\mathbf{Dist}\bigl(f(I_{adv}),\,f(I)\bigr)\quad\text{s.t.}\quad\|I_{adv}-I\|_{\infty}\leq\delta,$$  |  | (13)
L549: where $\mathbf{Dist}$ denotes a distance measure, and $\delta$ is the perturbation budget controlling the maximum allowable pixel-wise deviation. After optimizing cite263†Eq. 13 , $I_{adv}$ is likely to induce failure in watermark extraction. In our experiment, we instantiate embedding attack using two encoders, ResNet-18 [cite122†17 ] and CLIP [cite115†39 ]. As shown in cite262†Tab. 19 , OSI still outperforms GS across all settings.
